WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.274 --> 00:00:05.693
Welcome everybody to
another YouTube Hangout.

00:00:05.693 --> 00:00:07.453
So excited to be here.

00:00:07.453 --> 00:00:09.454
I've got a special guest
who's all fired up.

00:00:09.454 --> 00:00:13.377
Who woke up, it's five a.m.
for her, to do this interview.

00:00:13.377 --> 00:00:14.266
So I'm super pumped.

00:00:14.266 --> 00:00:17.491
It's Kate O'Brien, from
katemarieobrien.com.

00:00:17.491 --> 00:00:19.450
You can check her out in
the description below.

00:00:19.450 --> 00:00:21.708
She's going to interview me about

00:00:21.708 --> 00:00:23.608
success and entrepreneurship,

00:00:23.608 --> 00:00:25.386
and the bits that people don't talk about.

00:00:25.386 --> 00:00:27.594
But before I get into that
interview and the questions,

00:00:27.594 --> 00:00:30.066
Kate, maybe a quick intro for my audience

00:00:30.066 --> 00:00:31.525
so they know a little
bit about who you are

00:00:31.525 --> 00:00:32.690
and what you're all about.

00:00:32.690 --> 00:00:33.523
- Yep, hey everyone.

00:00:33.523 --> 00:00:34.356
Lovely to meet you.

00:00:34.356 --> 00:00:35.651
So, Kate O'brien.

00:00:35.651 --> 00:00:39.030
I have, you can find me
on my YouTube channel,

00:00:39.030 --> 00:00:41.099
on any social media.

00:00:41.099 --> 00:00:42.036
One thing I really love to do,

00:00:42.036 --> 00:00:45.363
and it's about pushing people's thinking

00:00:45.363 --> 00:00:47.297
and the way the relate to themselves,

00:00:47.297 --> 00:00:49.158
and the way they relate to each other,

00:00:49.158 --> 00:00:51.911
and I suppose I do that in
slightly controversial ways.

00:00:51.911 --> 00:00:54.878
Ways in which has people kind of poking

00:00:54.878 --> 00:00:57.211
on their own thinking, yeah.

00:00:59.365 --> 00:01:00.919
So come over to my YouTube channel,

00:01:00.919 --> 00:01:03.685
youtube.com/katemareeobrien.

00:01:03.685 --> 00:01:05.807
There's some videos there that could

00:01:05.807 --> 00:01:07.849
possibly even challenge you a little.

00:01:07.849 --> 00:01:08.682
- I love it.

00:01:08.682 --> 00:01:12.849
And the Maree is spelled in
an awesome way, M A R E E.

00:01:13.896 --> 00:01:14.905
- Yes.

00:01:14.905 --> 00:01:17.738
- Cool, it's your show, fire away.

00:01:19.793 --> 00:01:23.272
- Thank you, and also for
those of you who are watching

00:01:23.272 --> 00:01:24.877
from the Game Changer
Global Summit, huge welcome.

00:01:24.877 --> 00:01:27.858
Evan Carmichael, I just want to say

00:01:27.858 --> 00:01:29.177
it's a pleasure to meet you,

00:01:29.177 --> 00:01:31.568
and I've been watching
your YouTube channel,

00:01:31.568 --> 00:01:32.854
and I love what you put together,

00:01:32.854 --> 00:01:36.046
and I just love how you
clearly have been a guy

00:01:36.046 --> 00:01:40.162
who's gone all in and been
willing to have a vision

00:01:40.162 --> 00:01:42.386
and then really grow
something to match that.

00:01:42.386 --> 00:01:43.993
And I had a little bit of read of

00:01:43.993 --> 00:01:45.907
who you are and how you started,

00:01:45.907 --> 00:01:47.472
and I loved the story of,

00:01:47.472 --> 00:01:50.422
so you're five years
old with your sister...

00:01:50.422 --> 00:01:51.418
- Yeah.

00:01:51.418 --> 00:01:53.716
- And you guys are selling artwork.

00:01:53.716 --> 00:01:58.107
This is a guy with born
entrepreneurial spirit.

00:01:58.107 --> 00:01:59.881
Can I ask you first up,

00:01:59.881 --> 00:02:01.591
because I know you're all about

00:02:01.591 --> 00:02:03.212
helping entrepreneurs in the world,

00:02:03.212 --> 00:02:07.021
what made you such an
entrepreneurial little spark

00:02:07.021 --> 00:02:08.274
at the age of five?

00:02:08.274 --> 00:02:11.215
Was is a nature nurture thing?

00:02:11.215 --> 00:02:12.798
- Uh, I don't know.

00:02:13.893 --> 00:02:14.726
That's an interesting one.

00:02:14.726 --> 00:02:15.884
So, the story quickly.

00:02:15.884 --> 00:02:16.814
I was five years old.

00:02:16.814 --> 00:02:19.576
I grabbed my three year old little sister,

00:02:19.576 --> 00:02:21.824
and we both just started making drawings

00:02:21.824 --> 00:02:22.657
on a piece of paper,

00:02:22.657 --> 00:02:25.088
and I went to my neighbors
and started knocking on doors

00:02:25.088 --> 00:02:26.262
and selling it to them,

00:02:26.262 --> 00:02:29.505
and I made my first 10
cents as an entrepreneur.

00:02:29.505 --> 00:02:32.338
My parents were not entrepreneurs.

00:02:33.322 --> 00:02:35.065
They had stable jobs.

00:02:35.065 --> 00:02:38.427
My dad worked for the
government for years and years

00:02:38.427 --> 00:02:39.593
and eventually retired,

00:02:39.593 --> 00:02:41.247
and then got another job.

00:02:41.247 --> 00:02:43.580
So, I didn't have my parents

00:02:45.194 --> 00:02:46.941
to look up to for entrepreneurship,

00:02:46.941 --> 00:02:50.712
but they always did
encourage all three of us,

00:02:50.712 --> 00:02:51.594
I have two sisters,

00:02:51.594 --> 00:02:54.047
to do whatever we wanted,

00:02:54.047 --> 00:02:55.658
and make us believe that we could do

00:02:55.658 --> 00:02:57.042
whatever we wanted to do,

00:02:57.042 --> 00:02:58.375
and that really,

00:02:59.416 --> 00:03:03.061
all three of us kids went
off to do crazy things.

00:03:03.061 --> 00:03:04.990
Way different from each other,

00:03:04.990 --> 00:03:06.689
and way different than our parents,

00:03:06.689 --> 00:03:10.497
and went off to achieve a lot more, too,

00:03:10.497 --> 00:03:12.675
and so I don't know.

00:03:12.675 --> 00:03:14.106
I think the entrepreneurship thing

00:03:14.106 --> 00:03:16.268
didn't really come from them necessarily,

00:03:16.268 --> 00:03:20.055
but the encouragement
and guidance and belief

00:03:20.055 --> 00:03:22.468
and letting us experiment and try and fail

00:03:22.468 --> 00:03:26.385
and not judge us for
doing something different.

00:03:27.382 --> 00:03:31.824
I remember my parents when
to a parent-teacher meeting,

00:03:31.824 --> 00:03:33.969
I forget how old I was, I was super young,

00:03:33.969 --> 00:03:35.923
and I got a bad mark in art class,

00:03:35.923 --> 00:03:38.750
and my teachers gave some instructions

00:03:38.750 --> 00:03:40.973
about drawing a window with a tree,

00:03:40.973 --> 00:03:44.992
and I did it, but then I
added some other stuff to it,

00:03:44.992 --> 00:03:48.327
and my teacher gave me a
bad mark because I didn't

00:03:48.327 --> 00:03:50.624
follow her instructions exactly,

00:03:50.624 --> 00:03:52.153
and then my mom got into a fight with her,

00:03:52.153 --> 00:03:53.745
said hey, you said a window with a tree,

00:03:53.745 --> 00:03:54.894
that's what he gave you,

00:03:54.894 --> 00:03:56.071
and then he gave you more,

00:03:56.071 --> 00:03:58.435
so you can't blame him for that.

00:03:58.435 --> 00:04:00.242
So, like standing up to my teachers

00:04:00.242 --> 00:04:02.216
and just always encouraging me

00:04:02.216 --> 00:04:05.007
to do whatever I wanted do do.

00:04:05.007 --> 00:04:07.809
So, definitely I think the
parenting really helped me

00:04:07.809 --> 00:04:09.631
in that role, too.

00:04:09.631 --> 00:04:11.318
- Sounds like a lot of nurture as well.

00:04:11.318 --> 00:04:12.151
So out of interest,

00:04:12.151 --> 00:04:13.838
and I'm saying this selfishly

00:04:13.838 --> 00:04:15.991
because I'm a parent and I'm
assuming there's going to be

00:04:15.991 --> 00:04:18.191
a lot of parents on our
platform who are watching us,

00:04:18.191 --> 00:04:20.570
do you have kids, Evan?

00:04:20.570 --> 00:04:22.087
- Yeah, I have a seven year old.

00:04:22.087 --> 00:04:23.535
I don't know if you can
see in the video here.

00:04:23.535 --> 00:04:24.954
- Ah, yeah.

00:04:24.954 --> 00:04:27.434
- Little Hayden, so yeah.

00:04:27.434 --> 00:04:31.274
- How does that influence
how you bring up Hayden?

00:04:31.274 --> 00:04:33.775
- I'm a big believer that
everybody has one word

00:04:33.775 --> 00:04:34.917
that defines who they are.

00:04:34.917 --> 00:04:37.036
It's what the topic of
my new book is about,

00:04:37.036 --> 00:04:39.987
and once when you understand who you are,

00:04:39.987 --> 00:04:41.212
then you can start to live a life

00:04:41.212 --> 00:04:43.398
and have a business
that's more on purpose.

00:04:43.398 --> 00:04:44.984
And so believe is my one word.

00:04:44.984 --> 00:04:46.951
It's what I got from my parents.

00:04:46.951 --> 00:04:49.389
They always told me that I
was Evan Castrilli Carmichael,

00:04:49.389 --> 00:04:51.381
and I could do anything that I wanted.

00:04:51.381 --> 00:04:52.784
Even when I wasn't successful,

00:04:52.784 --> 00:04:54.518
even when I was getting bad report cards,

00:04:54.518 --> 00:04:56.689
even, you know, I got into trouble,

00:04:56.689 --> 00:04:58.062
sure I got punished,

00:04:58.062 --> 00:05:00.729
but also I was made to feel like

00:05:02.414 --> 00:05:03.669
I could do whatever I wanted.

00:05:03.669 --> 00:05:04.515
I could do better.

00:05:04.515 --> 00:05:05.970
I could always push myself.

00:05:05.970 --> 00:05:07.931
And so I continue that
same tradition with my son.

00:05:07.931 --> 00:05:10.489
I think the best gift
that I could give him

00:05:10.489 --> 00:05:12.844
is for him to believe in himself

00:05:12.844 --> 00:05:15.545
and not just follow in my footsteps

00:05:15.545 --> 00:05:17.676
or somebody else's footsteps.

00:05:17.676 --> 00:05:19.406
Maybe he doesn't want YouTube career

00:05:19.406 --> 00:05:20.498
or an entrepreneurship career.

00:05:20.498 --> 00:05:21.651
That's cool.

00:05:21.651 --> 00:05:24.878
Right now he wants to be a scientist,

00:05:24.878 --> 00:05:26.705
so great, like you could do science.

00:05:26.705 --> 00:05:28.095
Let's go learn some science,

00:05:28.095 --> 00:05:29.215
and when I pick him up from school,

00:05:29.215 --> 00:05:32.150
we have this little
tradition were I will say

00:05:32.150 --> 00:05:34.937
I'm Hayden Carmichael, that's his name,

00:05:34.937 --> 00:05:36.617
I'm Hayden Carmichael and I can do,

00:05:36.617 --> 00:05:40.159
and in the backseat he will go, anything.

00:05:40.159 --> 00:05:44.326
And so I think my parents
gave me that gift of believe,

00:05:46.207 --> 00:05:48.938
and I want to pass that on,
that believe, that support,

00:05:48.938 --> 00:05:52.839
that appreciation, that
non-judgement to my son

00:05:52.839 --> 00:05:56.423
and make him feel like he
can do anything he wants to.

00:05:56.423 --> 00:05:57.346
- I can feel that from you.

00:05:57.346 --> 00:05:58.311
It's actually interesting, Evan.

00:05:58.311 --> 00:06:01.894
I've had the total
pleasure of interviewing

00:06:04.321 --> 00:06:06.806
a lot of the big names in
the industry on this platform

00:06:06.806 --> 00:06:08.093
over the last four years,

00:06:08.093 --> 00:06:09.050
and I got to say,

00:06:09.050 --> 00:06:10.515
sitting here talking with you,

00:06:10.515 --> 00:06:11.503
you're almost exactly,

00:06:11.503 --> 00:06:13.135
you're pretty much exactly what I thought

00:06:13.135 --> 00:06:14.070
you were going to feel like.

00:06:14.070 --> 00:06:17.112
You are super, if you don't
mind me saying, super grounded.

00:06:17.112 --> 00:06:20.234
You've got this real, you've
got this very realness to you,

00:06:20.234 --> 00:06:21.439
which is interesting given

00:06:21.439 --> 00:06:23.819
the massive success of your audience,

00:06:23.819 --> 00:06:26.562
and how much you've grown your business.

00:06:26.562 --> 00:06:30.028
Can you tell me bit about
how have you managed

00:06:30.028 --> 00:06:34.104
to keep this grounded, human
feel that you really do have

00:06:34.104 --> 00:06:35.631
given the size of your success?

00:06:35.631 --> 00:06:36.747
Because you've been extremely successful,

00:06:36.747 --> 00:06:37.719
and it's very easy,

00:06:37.719 --> 00:06:39.113
I'm just going to segway into this

00:06:39.113 --> 00:06:40.189
while you're kind of thinking...

00:06:40.189 --> 00:06:41.150
- Sure.

00:06:41.150 --> 00:06:44.284
- Is that when people often
get success and get fame,

00:06:44.284 --> 00:06:46.370
sometimes people can
actually get carried away

00:06:46.370 --> 00:06:48.104
with the ideas of who they are,

00:06:48.104 --> 00:06:49.363
and then sometimes they can kind of

00:06:49.363 --> 00:06:52.007
loose a little bit of touch of the ground.

00:06:52.007 --> 00:06:55.257
I don't feel that from you at all, how?

00:06:57.260 --> 00:07:00.605
- Uh, I don't know, I don't know.

00:07:00.605 --> 00:07:01.438
That's interesting.

00:07:01.438 --> 00:07:02.938
I mean, I, thank you.

00:07:02.938 --> 00:07:05.521
I try to channel my core values

00:07:07.782 --> 00:07:10.199
through everything that I do,

00:07:11.135 --> 00:07:15.302
and I think it also kind of
depends on what I'm doing.

00:07:16.300 --> 00:07:19.144
I can be very confident in what I'm doing.

00:07:19.144 --> 00:07:20.157
I can turn up.

00:07:20.157 --> 00:07:21.084
I can get angry.

00:07:21.084 --> 00:07:23.233
If you want to debate something,

00:07:23.233 --> 00:07:25.937
and I'm convinced this is the path to go,

00:07:25.937 --> 00:07:29.415
not that it's going to
be demeaning or attacking

00:07:29.415 --> 00:07:30.998
or anything to you.

00:07:33.123 --> 00:07:35.172
So I can be really confident,

00:07:35.172 --> 00:07:36.606
and I can also be really humble

00:07:36.606 --> 00:07:39.528
just depending on the situation.

00:07:39.528 --> 00:07:41.181
I think one of the things
that has really helped me

00:07:41.181 --> 00:07:43.314
is just gratitude on a daily basis.

00:07:43.314 --> 00:07:45.557
One of the things that I do,

00:07:45.557 --> 00:07:47.690
I've had a gratitude practice
for a number of years

00:07:47.690 --> 00:07:50.997
where I just think about three
things that I'm grateful for,

00:07:50.997 --> 00:07:54.914
and I think if you can
remember the great stuff

00:07:56.408 --> 00:07:58.349
that you have in your life,

00:07:58.349 --> 00:08:00.009
and be thankful for it,

00:08:00.009 --> 00:08:01.223
then when the bad stuff happens,

00:08:01.223 --> 00:08:03.658
it doesn't overwhelm you.

00:08:03.658 --> 00:08:06.146
It's really hard to be
super angry and upset

00:08:06.146 --> 00:08:08.514
and depressed and down in dark places

00:08:08.514 --> 00:08:11.205
when you're also grateful
for what you have.

00:08:11.205 --> 00:08:12.835
And I think as entrepreneurs we need that.

00:08:12.835 --> 00:08:16.184
For the entrepreneurs who
are watching and listening,

00:08:16.184 --> 00:08:20.165
it's a lot of down days being
an entrepreneur, you know.

00:08:20.165 --> 00:08:21.338
You can have a lot of success, too,

00:08:21.338 --> 00:08:22.945
but there's still going
to be a lot of down days,

00:08:22.945 --> 00:08:27.112
and I think the gratitude
helps you stay grounded.

00:08:28.286 --> 00:08:32.593
I think success and fame or
whatever you want to call it,

00:08:32.593 --> 00:08:36.280
I think it just exposes more
of who you are honestly.

00:08:36.280 --> 00:08:38.457
I've meet a lot of people who
are at the top of their game

00:08:38.457 --> 00:08:41.183
who are really genuine and personable

00:08:41.183 --> 00:08:43.600
and just nice people as well,

00:08:45.915 --> 00:08:49.611
so I know I still have a long way to go

00:08:49.611 --> 00:08:52.914
from where I want to be
and where I'm at right now,

00:08:52.914 --> 00:08:56.997
and so I think the people
who have a big attitude

00:08:58.496 --> 00:08:59.329
when they're famous,

00:08:59.329 --> 00:09:01.272
they had a big attitude
when they weren't so famous.

00:09:01.272 --> 00:09:02.427
They weren't a great person

00:09:02.427 --> 00:09:04.073
when they were not so famous, too.

00:09:04.073 --> 00:09:06.530
And the fame and money and success

00:09:06.530 --> 00:09:09.947
just maybe exposes more of who they were.

00:09:11.032 --> 00:09:11.865
So I appreciate you saying that.

00:09:11.865 --> 00:09:15.615
My parents would be
super happy to hear that.

00:09:17.429 --> 00:09:19.753
Yeah, and I think both of those things

00:09:19.753 --> 00:09:20.884
just exposes more of who you are

00:09:20.884 --> 00:09:23.212
and then just being
grateful for what you have

00:09:23.212 --> 00:09:25.041
makes a big difference.

00:09:25.041 --> 00:09:26.031
- Yeah, I like what you said there,

00:09:26.031 --> 00:09:30.198
I think it really magnifies
what's in there, thank you.

00:09:31.163 --> 00:09:32.003
You've mentioned this before,

00:09:32.003 --> 00:09:34.931
where your talking about entrepreneurship,

00:09:34.931 --> 00:09:37.796
this, the days where there's highs

00:09:37.796 --> 00:09:39.600
and there's the real
successes that you can get,

00:09:39.600 --> 00:09:42.178
and then there's the days.

00:09:42.178 --> 00:09:43.026
Can you talk a little bit about that,

00:09:43.026 --> 00:09:44.989
'cause you know you hear
people talking about

00:09:44.989 --> 00:09:49.072
oh, wow, that was an overnight success,

00:09:49.072 --> 00:09:51.377
and you know often
people see the end result

00:09:51.377 --> 00:09:53.245
and the product of what
people have produced,

00:09:53.245 --> 00:09:54.511
and then relate to that as if

00:09:54.511 --> 00:09:56.509
that was their journey going in.

00:09:56.509 --> 00:09:58.795
But the reality is,

00:09:58.795 --> 00:10:00.459
my experience certainly has been

00:10:00.459 --> 00:10:03.459
it's the giving up a lot of my life.

00:10:04.722 --> 00:10:07.683
Not having, I haven't really had

00:10:07.683 --> 00:10:08.955
a social life in a long time.

00:10:08.955 --> 00:10:10.556
It just full commitment, you know.

00:10:10.556 --> 00:10:11.641
Can you talk a little bit about

00:10:11.641 --> 00:10:13.434
the reality of entrepreneurship

00:10:13.434 --> 00:10:16.552
and the parts that
possibly people don't see

00:10:16.552 --> 00:10:17.385
or don't relate to?

00:10:17.385 --> 00:10:21.823
'Cause what I see when
they're just relating

00:10:21.823 --> 00:10:23.588
to the end result,

00:10:23.588 --> 00:10:26.134
then when they are personally experiencing

00:10:26.134 --> 00:10:29.174
the not being able to
see immediate success,

00:10:29.174 --> 00:10:31.042
they then make themselves wrong,

00:10:31.042 --> 00:10:35.808
and then that byproduct
has them give up earlier

00:10:35.808 --> 00:10:37.226
and struggle through it.

00:10:37.226 --> 00:10:39.273
Not really getting it's the long haul,

00:10:39.273 --> 00:10:40.424
and it's the patience.

00:10:40.424 --> 00:10:42.071
Can you speak to a little bit, Evan?

00:10:42.071 --> 00:10:44.602
- Yeah, I think it's actually
one of the biggest disservices

00:10:44.602 --> 00:10:46.239
that we do to entrepreneurs.

00:10:46.239 --> 00:10:49.022
I think we celebrate
entrepreneurs and rightly so.

00:10:49.022 --> 00:10:50.767
I believe all the world's problems

00:10:50.767 --> 00:10:52.311
will be solved by entrepreneurs.

00:10:52.311 --> 00:10:55.225
It's not going to be solved
by government or corporations.

00:10:55.225 --> 00:10:56.994
It's going to be solved by us.

00:10:56.994 --> 00:11:00.411
You know, world peace, natural disasters,

00:11:02.637 --> 00:11:03.934
major health illnesses,

00:11:03.934 --> 00:11:06.417
it's going to be solved by entrepreneurs.

00:11:06.417 --> 00:11:08.563
And so we are right to celebrate them,

00:11:08.563 --> 00:11:12.637
but we don't showcase the
amount of work and pain

00:11:12.637 --> 00:11:15.090
that goes into making a business.

00:11:15.090 --> 00:11:17.423
And kudos to people like you

00:11:18.930 --> 00:11:19.939
who are putting on summits

00:11:19.939 --> 00:11:21.875
and trying to help spread education

00:11:21.875 --> 00:11:24.169
and give maybe a little dose of reality,

00:11:24.169 --> 00:11:26.966
'cause I think what ends up happening

00:11:26.966 --> 00:11:29.158
is one, too many people
jump into it too soon,

00:11:29.158 --> 00:11:30.690
who maybe shouldn't even be entrepreneurs,

00:11:30.690 --> 00:11:33.416
who just, they like the,

00:11:33.416 --> 00:11:35.510
they're attracted to the glam
and the fame and the glory,

00:11:35.510 --> 00:11:38.348
and the idea of making a lot of money,

00:11:38.348 --> 00:11:41.155
and maybe that's not what they
should be on the path to do.

00:11:41.155 --> 00:11:42.624
And also for the people who do start,

00:11:42.624 --> 00:11:45.207
what's most saddening for me is

00:11:46.181 --> 00:11:48.826
they feel isolated and alone.

00:11:48.826 --> 00:11:51.371
This is what I felt
when I was starting too.

00:11:51.371 --> 00:11:54.250
That when I look at websites,

00:11:54.250 --> 00:11:55.670
and when I look at magazines,

00:11:55.670 --> 00:11:56.568
and when I watch videos

00:11:56.568 --> 00:11:58.482
I see all these people
having so much success,

00:11:58.482 --> 00:12:01.565
and here I am grinding, working hard,

00:12:03.480 --> 00:12:04.939
thinking I'm doing the right thing,

00:12:04.939 --> 00:12:05.985
and I'm not getting results.

00:12:05.985 --> 00:12:07.960
Like, what's wrong with me?

00:12:07.960 --> 00:12:09.324
How come these guys are doing so well

00:12:09.324 --> 00:12:11.965
and I can't make my thing take off?

00:12:11.965 --> 00:12:13.562
Because it's suppose to be easy and

00:12:13.562 --> 00:12:15.002
it's suppose to be the path,

00:12:15.002 --> 00:12:16.444
and I'm not figuring it out.

00:12:16.444 --> 00:12:18.476
And it crushes a lot of people,

00:12:18.476 --> 00:12:22.643
and most people are too ashamed,
too afraid to admit that.

00:12:24.619 --> 00:12:27.758
You know, I was making $300
a month in my first business,

00:12:27.758 --> 00:12:29.436
and I couldn't afford to go out.

00:12:29.436 --> 00:12:30.799
I had to pick one thing a month

00:12:30.799 --> 00:12:32.670
that I could do with my friends,

00:12:32.670 --> 00:12:36.068
like a birthday, or beer,
pizza and beer, or something,

00:12:36.068 --> 00:12:38.824
'cause that $20 was a lot to me.

00:12:38.824 --> 00:12:39.657
- Um-Hum.

00:12:40.802 --> 00:12:43.487
- And I didn't have the
courage to tell my friends

00:12:43.487 --> 00:12:46.404
that I couldn't afford to be there.

00:12:48.612 --> 00:12:51.023
It was yeah, I'm living
the entrepreneur life.

00:12:51.023 --> 00:12:52.896
I've got so many things going on.

00:12:52.896 --> 00:12:54.375
I'm so busy, busy, busy, busy.

00:12:54.375 --> 00:12:55.783
I'm building my business.

00:12:55.783 --> 00:12:58.030
I wanted them to see me as,

00:12:58.030 --> 00:12:59.742
I wanted them to be jealous
of what I was doing.

00:12:59.742 --> 00:13:01.409
Not feel sad for me.

00:13:02.850 --> 00:13:05.517
(both laughing)

00:13:06.494 --> 00:13:08.157
I think that's a lot of entrepreneurs.

00:13:08.157 --> 00:13:09.958
We expect it to be easier than it is.

00:13:09.958 --> 00:13:12.217
Nobody's talking about how hard it is,

00:13:12.217 --> 00:13:13.895
and then we set ourselves up for failure,

00:13:13.895 --> 00:13:18.062
and we set ourselves up for
massive potential depression.

00:13:19.878 --> 00:13:21.787
You're so isolated and alone,

00:13:21.787 --> 00:13:23.891
and so I think talking
about how hard it is

00:13:23.891 --> 00:13:25.576
to be an entrepreneur,

00:13:25.576 --> 00:13:28.745
and recognizing that lots of
people think about quitting.

00:13:28.745 --> 00:13:31.361
I told my partner I quit,

00:13:31.361 --> 00:13:35.207
and it's normal to think
about quitting a business,

00:13:35.207 --> 00:13:36.893
especially in the early days.

00:13:36.893 --> 00:13:38.086
And a lot of people thought about it.

00:13:38.086 --> 00:13:39.419
So if you're thinking about
quitting your business,

00:13:39.419 --> 00:13:42.571
you know, you're out talking
to people and doing interviews,

00:13:42.571 --> 00:13:44.459
and saying yeah, everything's
great, I'm so happy.

00:13:44.459 --> 00:13:46.311
This business is all about x, y, z,

00:13:46.311 --> 00:13:47.341
and you're trying to promote.

00:13:47.341 --> 00:13:48.755
You're in hustle mode.

00:13:48.755 --> 00:13:49.588
Meanwhile, you think,

00:13:49.588 --> 00:13:52.035
I don't know if I want to keep doing this.

00:13:52.035 --> 00:13:53.132
Like, I feel bad about myself.

00:13:53.132 --> 00:13:54.036
I'm not getting results.

00:13:54.036 --> 00:13:55.323
Maybe I should quit.

00:13:55.323 --> 00:13:56.477
Who do you tell that you're thinking

00:13:56.477 --> 00:13:58.682
about quitting your business?

00:13:58.682 --> 00:14:00.270
A lot of people don't
have anybody to tell,

00:14:00.270 --> 00:14:02.155
and they think that they're abnormal,

00:14:02.155 --> 00:14:03.713
and they think that they're a failure,

00:14:03.713 --> 00:14:05.837
where it's totally normal,

00:14:05.837 --> 00:14:07.904
and it happens to almost everybody.

00:14:07.904 --> 00:14:09.725
And so just sharing that side of things,

00:14:09.725 --> 00:14:11.951
I think will help a lot of people.

00:14:11.951 --> 00:14:14.759
For people to feel like
this is part of it.

00:14:14.759 --> 00:14:16.154
To have that expectation,

00:14:16.154 --> 00:14:19.114
and to know that they are not alone,

00:14:19.114 --> 00:14:21.124
and that they are not worthless,

00:14:21.124 --> 00:14:23.807
and that this is part of the path.

00:14:23.807 --> 00:14:27.295
I think that will make it
a lot easier for people.

00:14:27.295 --> 00:14:29.656
For them to have success,

00:14:29.656 --> 00:14:31.413
and also for them to feel
good about themselves

00:14:31.413 --> 00:14:32.273
and what they're doing.

00:14:32.273 --> 00:14:35.364
And so I think the work
you're doing is great.

00:14:35.364 --> 00:14:36.427
To be able to ask these
kinds of questions,

00:14:36.427 --> 00:14:37.851
have these speakers on,

00:14:37.851 --> 00:14:40.684
and not just talk about
winning all the time,

00:14:40.684 --> 00:14:42.251
but some of the things that go into it,

00:14:42.251 --> 00:14:45.969
and the hard journey
that people have to take.

00:14:45.969 --> 00:14:47.283
- Thanks Evan, I appreciate that,

00:14:47.283 --> 00:14:48.457
and I also appreciate your answer.

00:14:48.457 --> 00:14:49.643
And for whoever is listening,

00:14:49.643 --> 00:14:52.624
I just want, if you hear
anything in this interview,

00:14:52.624 --> 00:14:55.010
I would love it if you
just really got that.

00:14:55.010 --> 00:14:56.163
Like got it on a cellular level

00:14:56.163 --> 00:14:57.890
that it takes something,

00:14:57.890 --> 00:15:00.130
and that it's part of the
journey when you're feel like,

00:15:00.130 --> 00:15:02.203
on one hand, you're putting
out the smiley face,

00:15:02.203 --> 00:15:03.174
but on the other hand,

00:15:03.174 --> 00:15:04.751
you're rocking in the
corner sucking your thumb.

00:15:04.751 --> 00:15:06.754
That that can be a part of it,

00:15:06.754 --> 00:15:08.735
and that doesn't mean anything.

00:15:08.735 --> 00:15:10.978
You talked about the
future of entrepreneurship,

00:15:10.978 --> 00:15:12.679
which really fascinates me.

00:15:12.679 --> 00:15:15.032
I'm really curious, what do you see, Evan,

00:15:15.032 --> 00:15:16.355
as the future of entrepreneurship?

00:15:16.355 --> 00:15:18.914
I mean, the world is
changing astronomically

00:15:18.914 --> 00:15:22.044
like we've never seen in history,

00:15:22.044 --> 00:15:24.839
and I know it's forecasting,
crystal ball stuff,

00:15:24.839 --> 00:15:27.714
but what do you see with the
future of entrepreneurship?

00:15:27.714 --> 00:15:29.841
- I think more and more
people are going to do it.

00:15:29.841 --> 00:15:33.765
I think everybody, I think
everybody has always had an idea

00:15:33.765 --> 00:15:35.427
for something that they want to do.

00:15:35.427 --> 00:15:37.572
I think if you go back 100 years,

00:15:37.572 --> 00:15:39.912
people are still thinking,
ah I wish I could do that.

00:15:39.912 --> 00:15:43.245
I have this idea for this new invention.

00:15:44.378 --> 00:15:48.680
I think it's just way easier now to do it.

00:15:48.680 --> 00:15:51.597
Both from a money point of view,

00:15:51.597 --> 00:15:54.233
100 years ago to start a business

00:15:54.233 --> 00:15:55.837
you needed to have a lot of money,

00:15:55.837 --> 00:15:57.995
you needed to have a factory and a store,

00:15:57.995 --> 00:15:59.725
and it cost you a lot
of money to get started,

00:15:59.725 --> 00:16:01.304
where now you could just,

00:16:01.304 --> 00:16:02.746
you could be a YouTube entrepreneur,

00:16:02.746 --> 00:16:04.458
you could be an Instagram entrepreneur,

00:16:04.458 --> 00:16:06.934
you can just get started from your phone.

00:16:06.934 --> 00:16:09.233
You don't need to have
money to get started.

00:16:09.233 --> 00:16:11.828
So because those barriers are going down,

00:16:11.828 --> 00:16:13.859
it's easier for people to get into it.

00:16:13.859 --> 00:16:16.109
I think too, now as you see

00:16:17.693 --> 00:16:21.661
more and more different kinds
of people being successful,

00:16:21.661 --> 00:16:25.340
then there's more role
models to look up to.

00:16:25.340 --> 00:16:28.363
So, Zuckerberg making it
big as a young entrepreneur

00:16:28.363 --> 00:16:30.256
inspired a lot of other
young entrepreneurs

00:16:30.256 --> 00:16:31.192
to say hey, look what he did,

00:16:31.192 --> 00:16:32.548
I could do that too.

00:16:32.548 --> 00:16:34.701
You don't have to wait
until you're 40, 50, 60

00:16:34.701 --> 00:16:36.751
to do your own thing.

00:16:36.751 --> 00:16:39.873
I'm 19, I want to go do it right now,

00:16:39.873 --> 00:16:42.132
and as you see entrepreneurs
breaking through

00:16:42.132 --> 00:16:43.449
and having those successes,

00:16:43.449 --> 00:16:47.616
they become role models
for the next generation.

00:16:47.616 --> 00:16:50.545
And you see that across all industries.

00:16:50.545 --> 00:16:53.238
I'm a baseball fan, so Jackie Robinson

00:16:53.238 --> 00:16:56.702
was the first black
baseball player in baseball,

00:16:56.702 --> 00:16:57.889
and after he made it,

00:16:57.889 --> 00:17:01.664
they wanted to aspire to be like Jackie.

00:17:01.664 --> 00:17:03.901
Now, most of baseball,

00:17:03.901 --> 00:17:05.914
your going to have tons of black players.

00:17:05.914 --> 00:17:07.081
It's the norm.

00:17:09.102 --> 00:17:12.006
And so when you see somebody
like you hitting it,

00:17:12.006 --> 00:17:13.924
then you feel like you could do it too,

00:17:13.924 --> 00:17:17.034
and so there's so many different
kinds of entrepreneurs now

00:17:17.034 --> 00:17:19.330
having success that
inspires the next generation

00:17:19.330 --> 00:17:21.198
to want to go out and do more.

00:17:21.198 --> 00:17:22.779
So I think, like look at what you're doing

00:17:22.779 --> 00:17:25.856
it wouldn't have been
possible 20 years ago.

00:17:25.856 --> 00:17:27.256
Here we are having a conversation

00:17:27.256 --> 00:17:31.873
from halfway across the
world talking business,

00:17:31.873 --> 00:17:36.104
and this would have cost an
exorbitant amount of money

00:17:36.104 --> 00:17:37.242
to put together before.

00:17:37.242 --> 00:17:38.660
Now we're able to do it

00:17:38.660 --> 00:17:42.214
just through a webcam and
an internet connection.

00:17:42.214 --> 00:17:43.502
And so people will see what you're doing

00:17:43.502 --> 00:17:44.767
and be inspired and say,

00:17:44.767 --> 00:17:47.219
hey, look what Kate did Mommy,

00:17:47.219 --> 00:17:48.052
look what Kate did.

00:17:48.052 --> 00:17:50.193
Like a 10 year old girl looking up to you

00:17:50.193 --> 00:17:51.519
saying I want to do that,

00:17:51.519 --> 00:17:52.741
I want to do something like that.

00:17:52.741 --> 00:17:54.814
And then she's inspired to want to go and

00:17:54.814 --> 00:17:56.308
bet on herself to do something.

00:17:56.308 --> 00:17:57.880
So I think people have always had,

00:17:57.880 --> 00:17:59.070
I think it's part of human nature.

00:17:59.070 --> 00:18:00.801
You're thinking about opportunities.

00:18:00.801 --> 00:18:02.973
You want to do something more.

00:18:02.973 --> 00:18:06.461
I think now with how
cheap it is to get started

00:18:06.461 --> 00:18:09.711
and how many role models to look up to,

00:18:11.317 --> 00:18:13.793
that we're only going to
see more and more people

00:18:13.793 --> 00:18:16.376
try and start different things.

00:18:17.607 --> 00:18:18.615
- I like that you said just before,

00:18:18.615 --> 00:18:20.273
you talked about almost like, you know,

00:18:20.273 --> 00:18:22.225
one person kind of leads
the way with the torch,

00:18:22.225 --> 00:18:24.178
which opens the door for a lot of people

00:18:24.178 --> 00:18:25.755
who can look up to that person.

00:18:25.755 --> 00:18:28.375
I stayed up last night
watching some Will Smith docu-,

00:18:28.375 --> 00:18:29.867
not Will Smith, Will.I.Am.

00:18:29.867 --> 00:18:31.284
I love Will.I.Am.

00:18:32.523 --> 00:18:34.408
That he's just such an innovator,

00:18:34.408 --> 00:18:38.929
and it made me think
about Will.I.Am came from

00:18:38.929 --> 00:18:42.330
that area of the, you
know a harder upbringing,

00:18:42.330 --> 00:18:44.970
and he's totally opened the
door for people and led the way

00:18:44.970 --> 00:18:46.164
because he's into robotics,

00:18:46.164 --> 00:18:48.723
and he's brought that to that generation.

00:18:48.723 --> 00:18:49.822
Which is fantastic.

00:18:49.822 --> 00:18:51.519
And he said something really interesting.

00:18:51.519 --> 00:18:55.269
He said that you have
to have the willingness

00:18:56.652 --> 00:18:59.665
to fall flat on your
face over and over again

00:18:59.665 --> 00:19:02.780
and to constantly go to
places that you don't know.

00:19:02.780 --> 00:19:06.947
See, it's easy to be in the
background and be in your head,

00:19:07.887 --> 00:19:10.059
and keep saying the
same stuff to yourself,

00:19:10.059 --> 00:19:12.168
or keep having the same conversations,

00:19:12.168 --> 00:19:13.615
but if you actually jump out

00:19:13.615 --> 00:19:14.552
and go out on that skinny branch

00:19:14.552 --> 00:19:16.251
and you have those conversations

00:19:16.251 --> 00:19:17.870
that you don't know how to have,

00:19:17.870 --> 00:19:19.460
and do things that you
don't know how to do,

00:19:19.460 --> 00:19:21.471
and be willing to fall
on your face each time,

00:19:21.471 --> 00:19:23.352
then you're learning new stuff.

00:19:23.352 --> 00:19:24.679
What do you think about the willingness

00:19:24.679 --> 00:19:26.429
to fall on your face?

00:19:28.703 --> 00:19:30.305
- Yeah, I think most people have

00:19:30.305 --> 00:19:33.708
an unhealthy relationship with failure.

00:19:33.708 --> 00:19:36.341
I think people see failure
as this one final thing

00:19:36.341 --> 00:19:38.010
that then, because you didn't make it,

00:19:38.010 --> 00:19:40.096
that's it, you're done.

00:19:40.096 --> 00:19:41.580
And a lot people do that.

00:19:41.580 --> 00:19:42.598
A lot of people shrink down.

00:19:42.598 --> 00:19:45.134
A lot of people see an opportunity,

00:19:45.134 --> 00:19:46.767
take their shot, doesn't work out,

00:19:46.767 --> 00:19:47.600
and then they say,

00:19:47.600 --> 00:19:48.648
well that's, I took my shot,

00:19:48.648 --> 00:19:50.472
that's it, I'm done.

00:19:50.472 --> 00:19:51.739
And then they go back to the other life

00:19:51.739 --> 00:19:54.097
that they didn't like
working for somebody else

00:19:54.097 --> 00:19:56.772
or whatever it was that they wanted to do.

00:19:56.772 --> 00:19:58.643
And I think that's wrong.

00:19:58.643 --> 00:20:01.132
I think if you look at
any successful person,

00:20:01.132 --> 00:20:02.188
not even entrepreneur,

00:20:02.188 --> 00:20:05.188
a musician, athlete, whatever it is,

00:20:06.188 --> 00:20:08.355
they failed tons of times.

00:20:09.821 --> 00:20:11.345
They failed more than they succeeded,

00:20:11.345 --> 00:20:12.552
and that's why they succeed.

00:20:12.552 --> 00:20:15.030
So failure is just part of the path.

00:20:15.030 --> 00:20:18.230
Failure is part of the journey to success.

00:20:18.230 --> 00:20:21.779
And it's only really failure if you stop.

00:20:21.779 --> 00:20:24.602
If you learn from it, you can
find another way to do it.

00:20:24.602 --> 00:20:25.750
Like okay, that didn't work.

00:20:25.750 --> 00:20:28.065
Let's find another path and how to do,

00:20:28.065 --> 00:20:29.403
and each time you get a little bit better.

00:20:29.403 --> 00:20:32.316
So whoever you're, if
somebody's afraid of that,

00:20:32.316 --> 00:20:35.898
if somebody's really, they are
afraid to fall on their face,

00:20:35.898 --> 00:20:37.557
and that's why their not doing it,

00:20:37.557 --> 00:20:41.099
if somebody watches this interview,

00:20:41.099 --> 00:20:42.729
and looks up to what
you're doing and says,

00:20:42.729 --> 00:20:45.478
hey, I want to do an interview in my field

00:20:45.478 --> 00:20:47.403
of figure skating or whatever it is,

00:20:47.403 --> 00:20:49.205
but their too afraid to do it,

00:20:49.205 --> 00:20:52.102
I would go and look at whoever
is the most successful person

00:20:52.102 --> 00:20:54.375
that you respect and look up to.

00:20:54.375 --> 00:20:57.289
You know I have a bunch of
people on my wall behind me.

00:20:57.289 --> 00:20:58.931
Whoever it is for you,

00:20:58.931 --> 00:21:02.287
a figure skater, a musician,
Will.I.Am, a politician,

00:21:02.287 --> 00:21:05.819
and entrepreneur, you know,
an athlete, whoever it is,

00:21:05.819 --> 00:21:07.029
look at their story

00:21:07.029 --> 00:21:09.440
and look at how many times
they failed, and failed,

00:21:09.440 --> 00:21:12.125
and failed, and failed, and failed,

00:21:12.125 --> 00:21:13.673
and use that as inspiration.

00:21:13.673 --> 00:21:15.066
Especially if you feel
an emotional connection

00:21:15.066 --> 00:21:17.016
to that person, you know.

00:21:17.016 --> 00:21:19.020
Like I could have Howard
Schultz behind me.

00:21:19.020 --> 00:21:20.371
His story inspires me.

00:21:20.371 --> 00:21:22.334
You may not know who Howard Schultz is,

00:21:22.334 --> 00:21:23.279
so that's not going to,

00:21:23.279 --> 00:21:24.321
like don't do what I do,

00:21:24.321 --> 00:21:27.030
do something that's inspiring for you.

00:21:27.030 --> 00:21:28.947
And so I think there is

00:21:30.232 --> 00:21:32.600
an unhealthy relationship with failure.

00:21:32.600 --> 00:21:35.100
And I think alongside of that,

00:21:37.059 --> 00:21:38.413
one of the biggest things

00:21:38.413 --> 00:21:40.115
that people worry about with failure

00:21:40.115 --> 00:21:43.911
is not so much failure of it not working,

00:21:43.911 --> 00:21:45.329
it's the judgement of other people

00:21:45.329 --> 00:21:47.496
because you failed, right?

00:21:48.558 --> 00:21:50.100
I think if people could fail in private

00:21:50.100 --> 00:21:51.292
then they would be much more open to it

00:21:51.292 --> 00:21:53.552
than failing and your husband sees it

00:21:53.552 --> 00:21:58.143
or your friends see it, or your
people in community see it.

00:21:58.143 --> 00:22:01.726
And I think an amazing
skill to get used to

00:22:04.377 --> 00:22:07.793
is practicing not making decisions

00:22:07.793 --> 00:22:11.205
based off of expectations of other people.

00:22:11.205 --> 00:22:12.597
And the story that I
like to tell with this

00:22:12.597 --> 00:22:16.764
is my son, we've talked
about a little bit already...

00:22:18.127 --> 00:22:18.960
- [Kate] Hayden.

00:22:18.960 --> 00:22:21.037
- Yeah Hayden, good memory.

00:22:21.037 --> 00:22:23.631
- He likes to wear different color socks.

00:22:23.631 --> 00:22:24.508
That's his thing.

00:22:24.508 --> 00:22:25.736
(Kate laughing)

00:22:25.736 --> 00:22:27.237
He will never wear the same socks.

00:22:27.237 --> 00:22:29.519
You bunch them together,
he will take them apart.

00:22:29.519 --> 00:22:31.258
He has to wear two different color socks.

00:22:31.258 --> 00:22:32.675
That's his thing.

00:22:34.568 --> 00:22:35.672
I like it, I support it.

00:22:35.672 --> 00:22:36.505
Like I love it.

00:22:36.505 --> 00:22:38.986
I don't want to judge him and
say hey, wear the same socks.

00:22:38.986 --> 00:22:41.135
Like those are the things
sometimes parents do.

00:22:41.135 --> 00:22:41.968
Well like, why?

00:22:41.968 --> 00:22:44.298
Why is it actually important
that he wears the same socks?

00:22:44.298 --> 00:22:47.735
Is it really the fight
that we want to have?

00:22:47.735 --> 00:22:48.568
I encourage it.

00:22:48.568 --> 00:22:49.401
I love it.

00:22:50.431 --> 00:22:51.945
And I was moving to my new condo,

00:22:51.945 --> 00:22:55.133
and I was throwing away
some stuff as you move,

00:22:55.133 --> 00:22:56.802
and I had these mismatched socks,

00:22:56.802 --> 00:22:58.496
and I was all set to throw them away,

00:22:58.496 --> 00:22:59.619
and I thought, huh,

00:22:59.619 --> 00:23:00.972
Hayden likes to wear
different color socks,

00:23:00.972 --> 00:23:04.555
maybe I'm going to wear
some mismatched socks.

00:23:06.522 --> 00:23:09.626
And the first thought
that came into my head was

00:23:09.626 --> 00:23:11.804
oh, but what if people see me in my socks

00:23:11.804 --> 00:23:13.435
and they're not same color, you know.

00:23:13.435 --> 00:23:16.281
I go, maybe I'll do it at home,

00:23:16.281 --> 00:23:18.637
but if I go out, I have
different color socks on.

00:23:18.637 --> 00:23:19.970
That's not cool.

00:23:21.235 --> 00:23:23.972
And because I had that thought,

00:23:23.972 --> 00:23:27.300
then I had to go out and do it in public.

00:23:27.300 --> 00:23:28.731
Because I was so worried

00:23:28.731 --> 00:23:31.077
about how other people would judge me,

00:23:31.077 --> 00:23:32.267
then I had to go out and do it.

00:23:32.267 --> 00:23:35.501
To build the muscle to
stop making decisions

00:23:35.501 --> 00:23:39.668
based on how other people
would potentially see me.

00:23:41.586 --> 00:23:44.669
I would walk to, I'm an
owner of a dance studio

00:23:44.669 --> 00:23:45.502
here in Toronto.

00:23:45.502 --> 00:23:46.645
We teach Salsa.

00:23:46.645 --> 00:23:48.625
And I would walk to the studio,

00:23:48.625 --> 00:23:51.834
and most of the path is on side streets.

00:23:51.834 --> 00:23:54.141
And so I've got music playing in my ears,

00:23:54.141 --> 00:23:56.936
and I'm dancing, I'm like grooving out,

00:23:56.936 --> 00:23:59.188
I might be singing as I'm
walking down the street,

00:23:59.188 --> 00:24:01.767
and as I get closer to
the main intersection

00:24:01.767 --> 00:24:02.741
and there's people there,

00:24:02.741 --> 00:24:03.824
it's like oh.

00:24:04.951 --> 00:24:05.887
- Yeah, yeah.

00:24:05.887 --> 00:24:08.921
- I go back to like,
conforming with everybody else.

00:24:08.921 --> 00:24:10.098
And as soon as I caught that,

00:24:10.098 --> 00:24:11.306
Most of that is just subconscious.

00:24:11.306 --> 00:24:12.784
As soon as I caught that,

00:24:12.784 --> 00:24:14.150
like, why am I stopping?

00:24:14.150 --> 00:24:15.149
What do these people care?

00:24:15.149 --> 00:24:17.394
I'm not doing something
that is offending them.

00:24:17.394 --> 00:24:20.581
I'm not trying to make their
lives worse in any way.

00:24:20.581 --> 00:24:22.058
That's a different story.

00:24:22.058 --> 00:24:23.612
And so as soon as I caught that,

00:24:23.612 --> 00:24:25.716
like okay, now I need to
go to that intersection

00:24:25.716 --> 00:24:29.869
and dance on the street with my music on.

00:24:29.869 --> 00:24:31.349
I got to stop shrinking down

00:24:31.349 --> 00:24:33.345
because of how somebody might judge me.

00:24:33.345 --> 00:24:35.782
And I think that's a
really important muscle

00:24:35.782 --> 00:24:37.255
to start to build for anybody,

00:24:37.255 --> 00:24:39.257
not just entrepreneurs, for anybody.

00:24:39.257 --> 00:24:42.567
That the reason you don't do something

00:24:42.567 --> 00:24:44.672
should never be because you are afraid

00:24:44.672 --> 00:24:46.697
of somebody's judgement.

00:24:46.697 --> 00:24:49.559
You want to do things
because you want to do them,

00:24:49.559 --> 00:24:51.121
and failure is okay,

00:24:51.121 --> 00:24:53.085
and it's part of the path to success,

00:24:53.085 --> 00:24:54.421
but if the reason is you're worried

00:24:54.421 --> 00:24:56.242
how somebody's going to look at you,

00:24:56.242 --> 00:24:57.842
it's not good enough,

00:24:57.842 --> 00:24:59.072
'cause you should care more about

00:24:59.072 --> 00:24:59.972
your opinion of yourself

00:24:59.972 --> 00:25:02.878
than other people's opinion of you.

00:25:02.878 --> 00:25:05.554
- I feel like going amen.

00:25:05.554 --> 00:25:06.785
(laughing)

00:25:06.785 --> 00:25:07.952
- Do it, amen!

00:25:09.043 --> 00:25:10.289
- Amen!

00:25:10.289 --> 00:25:12.458
(laughing)

00:25:12.458 --> 00:25:14.237
- I don't want to wake up my kids.

00:25:14.237 --> 00:25:15.179
They're sleeping, so.

00:25:15.179 --> 00:25:18.135
Mom, on you another
interview being loud again?

00:25:18.135 --> 00:25:19.761
- Well that's another thing.

00:25:19.761 --> 00:25:22.326
That's another muscle to start to build,

00:25:22.326 --> 00:25:24.632
and you've had a ton of practice with it,

00:25:24.632 --> 00:25:27.382
but for people listening, watching,

00:25:27.382 --> 00:25:29.155
when you catch yourself doing that,

00:25:29.155 --> 00:25:31.910
I feel like I should just do that.

00:25:31.910 --> 00:25:32.827
Just do it.

00:25:33.695 --> 00:25:34.528
I feel like yelling amen.

00:25:34.528 --> 00:25:35.361
Yell amen.

00:25:36.596 --> 00:25:39.723
Like if you go to a
seminar or a conference,

00:25:39.723 --> 00:25:43.211
or you're watching you know
Kate's next thing online,

00:25:43.211 --> 00:25:45.022
in your home office or whatever,

00:25:45.022 --> 00:25:46.423
and you say, I feel like yelling amen,

00:25:46.423 --> 00:25:47.717
like yell amen.

00:25:47.717 --> 00:25:48.868
Get used to doing that.

00:25:48.868 --> 00:25:52.400
Get used to trusting that
little voice inside your head,

00:25:52.400 --> 00:25:55.038
and stop shrinking down.

00:25:55.038 --> 00:25:58.200
- Yes, and I'm just
going to go again, amen.

00:25:58.200 --> 00:26:00.137
- There you go, I love it.

00:26:00.137 --> 00:26:01.532
- Very cool.

00:26:01.532 --> 00:26:04.343
I hope, if you don't mind me asking,

00:26:04.343 --> 00:26:05.863
can I ask you about, like,

00:26:05.863 --> 00:26:07.860
so you just talked about
that little thought

00:26:07.860 --> 00:26:10.656
of noticing how you shrink.

00:26:10.656 --> 00:26:13.232
How you've shrank yourself down.

00:26:13.232 --> 00:26:14.390
If you don't mind me asking,

00:26:14.390 --> 00:26:16.258
where does it show up now in your life

00:26:16.258 --> 00:26:17.785
in terms of business?

00:26:17.785 --> 00:26:20.342
Is there anything that
you like, that next level,

00:26:20.342 --> 00:26:22.518
'cause we've always got a next level,

00:26:22.518 --> 00:26:23.881
of what we want to push to,

00:26:23.881 --> 00:26:25.021
and I know someone like you,

00:26:25.021 --> 00:26:27.754
who you're clearing
connected into your passion,

00:26:27.754 --> 00:26:29.438
and so passionate people are always like,

00:26:29.438 --> 00:26:31.163
oh my god, it's the next level,

00:26:31.163 --> 00:26:32.471
it's like your hungry

00:26:32.471 --> 00:26:34.585
for whatever you can create in this world.

00:26:34.585 --> 00:26:36.186
So the next level for you,

00:26:36.186 --> 00:26:38.454
is there anything that
comes up for you now

00:26:38.454 --> 00:26:41.871
were you have to still build that muscle,

00:26:42.829 --> 00:26:44.626
and you notice the thought
of wanting to shrink down,

00:26:44.626 --> 00:26:48.022
and your like, alright
because of that I will?

00:26:48.022 --> 00:26:50.257
- Yeah, the biggest one that will help me

00:26:50.257 --> 00:26:52.349
get to the next level,

00:26:52.349 --> 00:26:53.794
I don't know it.

00:26:53.794 --> 00:26:56.796
'Cause I'm doing it subconsciously.

00:26:56.796 --> 00:26:57.629
And that's the thing.

00:26:57.629 --> 00:26:58.687
That's the hardest part.

00:26:58.687 --> 00:27:00.268
You are doing so many, we all are,

00:27:00.268 --> 00:27:01.624
doing so many things subconsciously

00:27:01.624 --> 00:27:02.593
that are holding ourselves back,

00:27:02.593 --> 00:27:05.000
and we just do it out of routine.

00:27:05.000 --> 00:27:07.492
And we don't question or catch it enough.

00:27:07.492 --> 00:27:08.903
Once you start to catch it,

00:27:08.903 --> 00:27:11.299
then you can start to make changes.

00:27:11.299 --> 00:27:14.466
One of the biggest ones that I did was

00:27:16.546 --> 00:27:19.129
I like to give value to people,

00:27:20.073 --> 00:27:23.306
and I like to make sure that
their time is respected,

00:27:23.306 --> 00:27:26.710
and so that's a really good thing,

00:27:26.710 --> 00:27:28.456
and that I'm always early for a meeting,

00:27:28.456 --> 00:27:29.400
I'm always on time,

00:27:29.400 --> 00:27:34.029
I'm always prepared, if
anything over prepared,

00:27:34.029 --> 00:27:35.151
but the downside would be

00:27:35.151 --> 00:27:39.567
I would be too afraid
of not providing value,

00:27:39.567 --> 00:27:41.428
that I would say no to things,

00:27:41.428 --> 00:27:43.351
or I wouldn't take the opportunity

00:27:43.351 --> 00:27:47.310
or I would cancel potentially on people,

00:27:47.310 --> 00:27:50.667
or wish that it would get cancelled.

00:27:50.667 --> 00:27:51.500
- Um.

00:27:52.953 --> 00:27:55.041
- Where this is a thing that I love doing.

00:27:55.041 --> 00:27:56.472
Like I love talking to you.

00:27:56.472 --> 00:27:57.572
I love doing interviews.

00:27:57.572 --> 00:27:58.597
I love spreading the message.

00:27:58.597 --> 00:28:00.599
I love reaching more people.

00:28:00.599 --> 00:28:02.932
But the Evan of 10 years ago

00:28:04.256 --> 00:28:07.684
would've probably said
yes to doing this meeting,

00:28:07.684 --> 00:28:11.142
but then secretly as it comes up,

00:28:11.142 --> 00:28:12.307
like it's coming up in half an hour,

00:28:12.307 --> 00:28:16.614
like, oh, you know, I
hope Kate cancels on me.

00:28:16.614 --> 00:28:17.447
- Haha.

00:28:18.647 --> 00:28:21.380
- Like secretly I'm sabotaging myself.

00:28:21.380 --> 00:28:22.307
- Uh-Huh.

00:28:22.307 --> 00:28:24.042
- Where I know this is what I love to do.

00:28:24.042 --> 00:28:26.044
This is what I love doing,

00:28:26.044 --> 00:28:28.512
and so sometimes it makes
sense for a bigger thing,

00:28:28.512 --> 00:28:31.861
if I commit to a big speech
in front of an audience

00:28:31.861 --> 00:28:33.531
that's bigger than I've ever done before,

00:28:33.531 --> 00:28:35.296
it's going to be on national T.V.,

00:28:35.296 --> 00:28:37.154
it's natural to feel nervous

00:28:37.154 --> 00:28:40.149
because you've never done it before.

00:28:40.149 --> 00:28:42.924
You know, I would still feel anxious

00:28:42.924 --> 00:28:44.394
and if somebody would cancel on me,

00:28:44.394 --> 00:28:46.264
I would feel like okay, no worries,

00:28:46.264 --> 00:28:47.427
yeah, yeah, cool, great.

00:28:47.427 --> 00:28:48.903
We'll schedule another time.

00:28:48.903 --> 00:28:51.236
Don't worry about it at all.

00:28:52.359 --> 00:28:54.827
So it makes sense for the big
things that you're scared of.

00:28:54.827 --> 00:28:57.072
For me it would happen on
the smallest of occasions.

00:28:57.072 --> 00:28:59.140
Like it would happen with an entrepreneur

00:28:59.140 --> 00:29:01.621
who wants to meet for coffee,

00:29:01.621 --> 00:29:03.590
and it's a start-up entrepreneur,

00:29:03.590 --> 00:29:04.906
and I know I can help her.

00:29:04.906 --> 00:29:06.644
And I've helped tons of people like her,

00:29:06.644 --> 00:29:07.754
and I love helping.

00:29:07.754 --> 00:29:10.192
I know I'll come out of the
meeting feeling so great

00:29:10.192 --> 00:29:12.789
that I was able to help this
person with their business,

00:29:12.789 --> 00:29:15.033
but going into it,

00:29:15.033 --> 00:29:16.984
I'm hoping that she's
going to cancel on me.

00:29:16.984 --> 00:29:19.029
And I remember there was
this one women in particular,

00:29:19.029 --> 00:29:20.479
it was this scenario.

00:29:20.479 --> 00:29:22.070
I was going to meet her
in a local coffee shop,

00:29:22.070 --> 00:29:23.186
and I was going to help
her with her business,

00:29:23.186 --> 00:29:27.522
and I was secretly hoping
that she would cancel on me,

00:29:27.522 --> 00:29:29.935
like I always do, or always did,

00:29:29.935 --> 00:29:31.322
and then I came out of the meeting,

00:29:31.322 --> 00:29:32.989
and I just sat down,

00:29:33.908 --> 00:29:35.249
and it was an amazing meeting.

00:29:35.249 --> 00:29:37.034
She was so grateful and so happy,

00:29:37.034 --> 00:29:40.129
and you could see that
she really made a shift,

00:29:40.129 --> 00:29:41.869
and she was going to
go off and apply this.

00:29:41.869 --> 00:29:43.974
And I felt so good about myself.

00:29:43.974 --> 00:29:46.194
And I remember thinking,

00:29:46.194 --> 00:29:48.217
I wanted this to cancel.

00:29:48.217 --> 00:29:51.026
Like this thing that gave
me such joy and energy,

00:29:51.026 --> 00:29:53.474
I wanted her to cancel on me.

00:29:53.474 --> 00:29:55.902
I was secretly praying
that it would cancel.

00:29:55.902 --> 00:29:57.971
I'm like, this isn't right, haha.

00:29:57.971 --> 00:30:00.957
Like, this is, that was
the moment where I realized

00:30:00.957 --> 00:30:03.493
that I was sabotaging myself.

00:30:03.493 --> 00:30:04.794
- Hmm.

00:30:04.794 --> 00:30:08.094
- And so the more I caught that behavior,

00:30:08.094 --> 00:30:09.862
we have an expression in North America,

00:30:09.862 --> 00:30:11.112
Monday morning quarterback,

00:30:11.112 --> 00:30:12.351
I don't know if this same thing,

00:30:12.351 --> 00:30:13.184
if that means anything,

00:30:13.184 --> 00:30:17.599
but basically a football
game is on Sunday,

00:30:17.599 --> 00:30:18.923
and so then the Monday
morning quarterback is,

00:30:18.923 --> 00:30:22.284
you think the next day of all
the things you could've done.

00:30:22.284 --> 00:30:24.512
Right, so like after the
presentation is over,

00:30:24.512 --> 00:30:27.913
oh, I should have done that,
and I should've you know.

00:30:27.913 --> 00:30:29.842
So, fine, you reflect on
what mistakes you made,

00:30:29.842 --> 00:30:31.601
and what you should've done.

00:30:31.601 --> 00:30:35.281
So, when you realize
that limiting behavior,

00:30:35.281 --> 00:30:36.930
then you'll do a lot of that.

00:30:36.930 --> 00:30:39.063
You'll do a lot of the next day thinking,

00:30:39.063 --> 00:30:42.309
ah, I should've, I did it again, right.

00:30:42.309 --> 00:30:43.439
I held myself back again.

00:30:43.439 --> 00:30:45.700
I should've done better that time.

00:30:45.700 --> 00:30:48.030
And then slowly you get
that closer to the point.

00:30:48.030 --> 00:30:49.492
So instead of it being the next day,

00:30:49.492 --> 00:30:51.196
it's only like three hours later,

00:30:51.196 --> 00:30:52.622
three hours after that
meeting that you realize.

00:30:52.622 --> 00:30:55.401
Then it's two hours, then
one hour, then 10 minutes,

00:30:55.401 --> 00:30:57.366
and you slowly get closer
and closer to that point

00:30:57.366 --> 00:31:01.625
where you made that
self-sabotaging potential decision.

00:31:01.625 --> 00:31:04.393
And then something happens
when you get that point.

00:31:04.393 --> 00:31:05.691
You realize in the moment that

00:31:05.691 --> 00:31:08.142
I'm sabotaging myself right now.

00:31:08.142 --> 00:31:09.949
This isn't the next day, looking back.

00:31:09.949 --> 00:31:11.033
It's happening right now.

00:31:11.033 --> 00:31:13.950
I'm about to sabotage myself again.

00:31:14.911 --> 00:31:17.385
And then having the
courage to do the thing

00:31:17.385 --> 00:31:20.510
that you're suppose to do,
but that you don't want to do.

00:31:20.510 --> 00:31:22.293
Having the courage to push through,

00:31:22.293 --> 00:31:25.458
and say, I'm not going to do
the thing that I always do,

00:31:25.458 --> 00:31:26.935
I'm going to take this path,

00:31:26.935 --> 00:31:29.074
because this is where I need to go.

00:31:29.074 --> 00:31:32.813
And once you get used to
doing that consistently,

00:31:32.813 --> 00:31:34.907
then you can really start to

00:31:34.907 --> 00:31:36.329
break down your limiting barriers,

00:31:36.329 --> 00:31:37.630
and really start to make progress.

00:31:37.630 --> 00:31:40.732
Every major growth that
I've had in my business

00:31:40.732 --> 00:31:43.885
has never come from a business strategy.

00:31:43.885 --> 00:31:46.325
It's come from me breaking through

00:31:46.325 --> 00:31:49.282
a limiting belief that I had,

00:31:49.282 --> 00:31:52.058
and then going out and
executing against it.

00:31:52.058 --> 00:31:53.419
- Amen.

00:31:53.419 --> 00:31:55.300
- Yeah, I love it.

00:31:55.300 --> 00:31:57.668
- This is stunning, Evan.

00:31:57.668 --> 00:31:59.446
And I just want to say
thank you for being willing

00:31:59.446 --> 00:32:00.920
to share all that,

00:32:00.920 --> 00:32:02.980
and I'm sure anyone who's listening

00:32:02.980 --> 00:32:04.498
is getting absolute gold from that.

00:32:04.498 --> 00:32:05.799
I just want to share a
little bit personally,

00:32:05.799 --> 00:32:07.507
because I know you don't know me

00:32:07.507 --> 00:32:09.898
and the audience most
likely doesn't know me,

00:32:09.898 --> 00:32:12.541
so I had massive social anxiety.

00:32:12.541 --> 00:32:17.101
So I was extremely, years
ago, social anxiety,

00:32:17.101 --> 00:32:20.829
I also had anorexia for
three years and depression.

00:32:20.829 --> 00:32:24.852
So you know, the person
you're talking to today

00:32:24.852 --> 00:32:27.951
feels very different from
the person I was years ago.

00:32:27.951 --> 00:32:29.783
In fact, total rock bottom.

00:32:29.783 --> 00:32:31.644
I remember standing at a checkout,

00:32:31.644 --> 00:32:33.510
at a supermarket checkout,

00:32:33.510 --> 00:32:36.475
to buy like a packet of gum or whatever,

00:32:36.475 --> 00:32:39.601
and standing there and I was forcing cue,

00:32:39.601 --> 00:32:43.890
and literally the amount
that my palms were sweating

00:32:43.890 --> 00:32:46.150
and my heart was racing 'cause
I literally had to speak

00:32:46.150 --> 00:32:49.565
to a human being at the checkout,

00:32:49.565 --> 00:32:51.285
and you know I was forcing cue,

00:32:51.285 --> 00:32:53.785
and each person that went down

00:32:54.835 --> 00:32:56.358
and it was counting down to me,

00:32:56.358 --> 00:32:57.771
I remember I felt like
I was going to vomit,

00:32:57.771 --> 00:33:00.847
'cause I had to speak to a human being.

00:33:00.847 --> 00:33:02.108
- Right.

00:33:02.108 --> 00:33:05.006
- And you know, what was
going through my head was

00:33:05.006 --> 00:33:06.078
how would I introduce myself.

00:33:06.078 --> 00:33:09.157
Would I say hi, would I
say hello, would I say hey.

00:33:09.157 --> 00:33:11.271
Those are my options, hi, hello, hey,

00:33:11.271 --> 00:33:13.419
and the intensity that went into that.

00:33:13.419 --> 00:33:14.252
And I got to say,

00:33:14.252 --> 00:33:16.006
the only thing that's got me from there,

00:33:16.006 --> 00:33:18.673
which is total, it was insanity,

00:33:20.025 --> 00:33:21.433
the experience that I had,

00:33:21.433 --> 00:33:25.697
to here where I said yes
to this live interview,

00:33:25.697 --> 00:33:28.978
is actually when I have
had these opportunities

00:33:28.978 --> 00:33:32.275
is learning to say yes
regardless of how I feel.

00:33:32.275 --> 00:33:33.175
It is the only way.

00:33:33.175 --> 00:33:34.638
I think sometimes people are,

00:33:34.638 --> 00:33:37.561
sorry, I think sometimes
people are wanting

00:33:37.561 --> 00:33:40.216
to wait until they feel that they can

00:33:40.216 --> 00:33:41.662
until they say yes to things.

00:33:41.662 --> 00:33:43.690
I think we have to switch
it the other way around.

00:33:43.690 --> 00:33:46.085
We have to say yes first.

00:33:46.085 --> 00:33:48.343
Which is why I'm so glad you're
having this conversation.

00:33:48.343 --> 00:33:49.786
Thanks, Evan.

00:33:49.786 --> 00:33:53.068
- Yeah, and especially when you
can commit to somebody else.

00:33:53.068 --> 00:33:53.901
- Yeah.

00:33:53.901 --> 00:33:56.297
- And especially when
it's out in the future.

00:33:56.297 --> 00:33:57.130
- [Kate] Yes.

00:33:57.130 --> 00:33:58.208
- Right, like yeah, yeah, yeah,

00:33:58.208 --> 00:33:59.427
we'll meet in three
months, sure, no problem.

00:33:59.427 --> 00:34:00.431
And then those three months come

00:34:00.431 --> 00:34:01.721
and then you don't want
to cancel on that person.

00:34:01.721 --> 00:34:03.152
Like, it's actually here.

00:34:03.152 --> 00:34:04.850
Okay, here we go.

00:34:04.850 --> 00:34:06.008
'Cause there's also the fear

00:34:06.008 --> 00:34:07.334
of looking bad in front of that person,

00:34:07.334 --> 00:34:10.882
so you want to, you're going to do it.

00:34:10.882 --> 00:34:11.715
Yeah, I agree.

00:34:11.715 --> 00:34:13.407
I think, ah,

00:34:13.407 --> 00:34:17.393
I'm a big believer in trying anything once

00:34:17.393 --> 00:34:20.494
just to get the experience, just to see.

00:34:20.494 --> 00:34:21.909
And if you're not going to do something,

00:34:21.909 --> 00:34:24.765
there should be a really
solid reason behind it,

00:34:24.765 --> 00:34:28.168
not just because you're afraid to do it.

00:34:28.168 --> 00:34:30.436
I'm just getting used
to building that habit.

00:34:30.436 --> 00:34:32.294
You know, I ask a lot of people,

00:34:32.294 --> 00:34:34.121
so whenever I do interviews with people,

00:34:34.121 --> 00:34:35.778
my default will be be,

00:34:35.778 --> 00:34:38.572
hey, how about we do it
as a YouTube interview.

00:34:38.572 --> 00:34:40.139
- Hm.

00:34:40.139 --> 00:34:41.108
- I want to promote you.

00:34:41.108 --> 00:34:42.721
How about we give you some free promotion.

00:34:42.721 --> 00:34:44.512
Does that sound like a good idea?

00:34:44.512 --> 00:34:45.537
Right.

00:34:45.537 --> 00:34:47.537
And some people say yes,

00:34:49.574 --> 00:34:51.328
and some people will say no.

00:34:51.328 --> 00:34:53.156
And usually the ones who say no

00:34:53.156 --> 00:34:55.477
are just too afraid to do it.

00:34:55.477 --> 00:34:59.542
And they'll come up with
a reason why they can't.

00:34:59.542 --> 00:35:02.582
Right, like oh, I don't have a webcam,

00:35:02.582 --> 00:35:04.435
so I can't do it.

00:35:04.435 --> 00:35:05.321
Well go buy a webcam.

00:35:05.321 --> 00:35:07.404
That's not a real reason.

00:35:08.264 --> 00:35:09.787
You're just afraid.

00:35:09.787 --> 00:35:11.979
And I don't push on somebody like that

00:35:11.979 --> 00:35:13.649
that I don't know.

00:35:13.649 --> 00:35:14.845
Right, if you didn't want to do this,

00:35:14.845 --> 00:35:17.371
I would have said, hey, you
want to do a Skype call?

00:35:17.371 --> 00:35:19.001
However you want to do, let's do it, Kate.

00:35:19.001 --> 00:35:20.418
I'm okay with it.

00:35:21.359 --> 00:35:24.026
But kudos to you for saying yes.

00:35:25.287 --> 00:35:27.418
- Yeah, literally this
was the one interview

00:35:27.418 --> 00:35:28.974
out of all the interviews that I've done

00:35:28.974 --> 00:35:31.266
that I was like, hmm, I felt it.

00:35:31.266 --> 00:35:33.286
I felt it when the opportunity was there,

00:35:33.286 --> 00:35:34.119
and I was like yeah.

00:35:34.119 --> 00:35:35.111
Literally what was in my mind

00:35:35.111 --> 00:35:37.695
was because it challenged me, I must.

00:35:37.695 --> 00:35:38.528
- Yeah.

00:35:38.528 --> 00:35:39.571
- So thank you for the opportunity

00:35:39.571 --> 00:35:41.151
to literally practice that muscle again

00:35:41.151 --> 00:35:44.166
in a different environment.

00:35:44.166 --> 00:35:44.999
- I love it.

00:35:44.999 --> 00:35:46.140
And the more you see that popping up,

00:35:46.140 --> 00:35:47.109
getting used to,

00:35:47.109 --> 00:35:48.474
it's same with the multicolored socks,

00:35:48.474 --> 00:35:49.993
it's the same with the
dancing on the street,

00:35:49.993 --> 00:35:52.974
why are you shrinking down from this?

00:35:52.974 --> 00:35:54.314
- Yeah.

00:35:54.314 --> 00:35:57.785
- Is there a legit reason or
is it just that you're afraid?

00:35:57.785 --> 00:35:59.470
And having the,

00:35:59.470 --> 00:36:01.455
you know we tell ourselves
really smart reasons

00:36:01.455 --> 00:36:03.209
why we can't do something.

00:36:03.209 --> 00:36:05.504
We're smart people and we tell,

00:36:05.504 --> 00:36:07.301
we come up with really convincing reasons

00:36:07.301 --> 00:36:09.468
why we can't do something.

00:36:11.599 --> 00:36:14.075
And getting used to calling yourself out

00:36:14.075 --> 00:36:15.888
on your own BS,

00:36:15.888 --> 00:36:18.721
to say listen, you're just afraid.

00:36:19.745 --> 00:36:20.745
Let's do it.

00:36:21.644 --> 00:36:22.988
- Hm.

00:36:22.988 --> 00:36:25.152
- Getting used to doing that

00:36:25.152 --> 00:36:26.439
becomes easier, and easier, and easier.

00:36:26.439 --> 00:36:28.541
Like any other muscle
that you try to build,

00:36:28.541 --> 00:36:30.386
becomes easier, and easier,
and easier, and easier.

00:36:30.386 --> 00:36:33.480
And so maybe the first thing
is something really small.

00:36:33.480 --> 00:36:34.494
If you have social anxiety,

00:36:34.494 --> 00:36:38.717
maybe it's saying yes to a
coffee meeting with somebody.

00:36:38.717 --> 00:36:40.004
Oh, I don't want to get out of my house,

00:36:40.004 --> 00:36:41.839
or oh, who's going to
be a the coffee shop,

00:36:41.839 --> 00:36:44.120
or whatever it is.

00:36:44.120 --> 00:36:47.181
It doesn't have to be some big huge goal

00:36:47.181 --> 00:36:48.259
where you're now speaking

00:36:48.259 --> 00:36:51.801
out in front of thousands of people.

00:36:51.801 --> 00:36:56.663
Just getting used to that
habit will take you so far

00:36:56.663 --> 00:36:58.055
in your business,

00:36:58.055 --> 00:36:59.388
but in you life.

00:37:02.427 --> 00:37:04.651
And it's connected to when
you think of something

00:37:04.651 --> 00:37:06.445
you want to say or you want to do,

00:37:06.445 --> 00:37:08.696
just taking some kind of action on it.

00:37:08.696 --> 00:37:11.381
Like, why not say amen and yell it.

00:37:11.381 --> 00:37:13.178
You know, okay, I'm going
to wake up the kids.

00:37:13.178 --> 00:37:15.244
That may be a legit reason, right.

00:37:15.244 --> 00:37:17.582
Like, I don't want to deal
with the kids at five a.m.

00:37:17.582 --> 00:37:19.483
When they're sleeping,

00:37:19.483 --> 00:37:23.066
but otherwise, why
would someone be afraid?

00:37:23.911 --> 00:37:26.820
Well, I'm on live camera.

00:37:26.820 --> 00:37:27.902
I don't want to say that.

00:37:27.902 --> 00:37:30.384
There's people watching,

00:37:30.384 --> 00:37:32.389
and that's not a great
reason for not to do it.

00:37:32.389 --> 00:37:35.035
So you stepped up again, and did it again.

00:37:35.035 --> 00:37:37.463
So I think it's one of
the most important lessons

00:37:37.463 --> 00:37:40.258
that people can learn
to start breaking down

00:37:40.258 --> 00:37:41.727
their barriers or limiting beliefs

00:37:41.727 --> 00:37:43.152
and start living a life that's

00:37:43.152 --> 00:37:46.264
a lot more meaningful for them.

00:37:46.264 --> 00:37:47.278
- Thank you.

00:37:47.278 --> 00:37:50.016
I just want to recap that for everyone.

00:37:50.016 --> 00:37:52.532
Noticing the areas where you
go to shrink yourself down,

00:37:52.532 --> 00:37:54.891
and be willing to actually go there anyway

00:37:54.891 --> 00:37:57.463
despite how you might feel on the inside.

00:37:57.463 --> 00:38:00.113
Evan, my husband, Henare and I,

00:38:00.113 --> 00:38:02.488
we actually are in business together.

00:38:02.488 --> 00:38:03.321
- Nice.

00:38:03.321 --> 00:38:07.321
- We talk a lot about the
conversational domains

00:38:08.968 --> 00:38:10.354
that we have within our life

00:38:10.354 --> 00:38:12.301
that we are not comfortable in.

00:38:12.301 --> 00:38:14.980
So you know how I can operate
in this area of my life,

00:38:14.980 --> 00:38:16.941
and like this kind of narrow strip here

00:38:16.941 --> 00:38:17.803
is where I'm really comfortable,

00:38:17.803 --> 00:38:19.050
and this might be,

00:38:19.050 --> 00:38:20.905
you know for some people this comfort zone

00:38:20.905 --> 00:38:22.634
might even be success, right?

00:38:22.634 --> 00:38:23.652
And the conversational domain

00:38:23.652 --> 00:38:26.335
that they're not comfortable in is family,

00:38:26.335 --> 00:38:28.246
or relationship, or relating to people.

00:38:28.246 --> 00:38:29.825
Or this conversational domain

00:38:29.825 --> 00:38:32.093
that I'm comfortable in is whatever.

00:38:32.093 --> 00:38:34.344
But then outside of that,

00:38:34.344 --> 00:38:37.152
all the places that
I'm not comfortable in,

00:38:37.152 --> 00:38:38.115
I'll just give a quick example.

00:38:38.115 --> 00:38:41.003
For example, if someone
is the nice person,

00:38:41.003 --> 00:38:42.263
and the good person,

00:38:42.263 --> 00:38:45.782
and they identify that as who they are,

00:38:45.782 --> 00:38:47.343
then perhaps a conversational domain

00:38:47.343 --> 00:38:50.764
that they're not comfortable
with could be their power.

00:38:50.764 --> 00:38:52.955
And so like in a situation where

00:38:52.955 --> 00:38:56.229
they are actually called
to be really shwoo,

00:38:56.229 --> 00:38:58.397
this is, to actually like,

00:38:58.397 --> 00:38:59.651
called to step up to the plate

00:38:59.651 --> 00:39:00.954
and bring some power to something

00:39:00.954 --> 00:39:03.057
might not be something that they can do

00:39:03.057 --> 00:39:04.643
because that's not who they are.

00:39:04.643 --> 00:39:06.018
Or sometimes conversational domains

00:39:06.018 --> 00:39:09.277
that I find a lot of people
can't go to is around sexuality.

00:39:09.277 --> 00:39:10.644
I know that's a little bit
outside of entrepreneurship,

00:39:10.644 --> 00:39:14.190
but it's a conversation of
where can people not go to,

00:39:14.190 --> 00:39:17.053
and that's actually literally for me,

00:39:17.053 --> 00:39:18.056
and I'll just go there first,

00:39:18.056 --> 00:39:20.781
I've been playing for
myself in two domains

00:39:20.781 --> 00:39:21.656
in the last couple of years

00:39:21.656 --> 00:39:22.981
because they are uncomfortable for me.

00:39:22.981 --> 00:39:24.473
One is my power,

00:39:24.473 --> 00:39:25.853
and then the other one is sexuality.

00:39:25.853 --> 00:39:26.985
I'm just curious for you,

00:39:26.985 --> 00:39:28.549
is perhaps a conversational domain,

00:39:28.549 --> 00:39:33.283
or an experiential domain
that you haven't been used to

00:39:33.283 --> 00:39:34.445
that you're perhaps not as comfortable in

00:39:34.445 --> 00:39:36.665
that you want, that you expand to,

00:39:36.665 --> 00:39:37.569
or you want to,

00:39:37.569 --> 00:39:38.736
or you try at?

00:39:40.797 --> 00:39:43.222
- That's an interesting topic.

00:39:43.222 --> 00:39:46.221
I have a couple of things to say on that.

00:39:46.221 --> 00:39:49.638
First, for me if there is something that,

00:39:50.884 --> 00:39:53.043
again, I feel like I want to learn and do

00:39:53.043 --> 00:39:55.951
and I feel is important
to where I'm growing,

00:39:55.951 --> 00:40:00.890
then I force myself into the
situation to be able to do it.

00:40:00.890 --> 00:40:02.358
Just like the dancing in
the street and the socks,

00:40:02.358 --> 00:40:03.912
or having a hard conversation,

00:40:03.912 --> 00:40:04.794
leading the team,

00:40:04.794 --> 00:40:06.174
or letting somebody go.

00:40:06.174 --> 00:40:08.591
I will force myself to do it.

00:40:09.846 --> 00:40:11.292
What's the reason why I'm not doing?

00:40:11.292 --> 00:40:12.179
Because I'm just afraid,

00:40:12.179 --> 00:40:14.262
so I'll go out and do it.

00:40:15.146 --> 00:40:16.659
There's probably lots of areas, again,

00:40:16.659 --> 00:40:18.937
that are subconscious that
I am restricting myself

00:40:18.937 --> 00:40:20.095
that I don't know about yet

00:40:20.095 --> 00:40:22.613
that I am yet to discover.

00:40:22.613 --> 00:40:24.773
But as soon as I do discover one,

00:40:24.773 --> 00:40:27.773
then I have to go out and attack it.

00:40:29.073 --> 00:40:29.906
- Hm.

00:40:29.906 --> 00:40:32.004
- I think what really helped me,

00:40:32.004 --> 00:40:33.126
and what could help a lot of people,

00:40:33.126 --> 00:40:36.384
is basically the topic of the book,

00:40:36.384 --> 00:40:39.268
is finding out the one
core value that you have

00:40:39.268 --> 00:40:42.465
to give yourself the self-awareness

00:40:42.465 --> 00:40:47.230
and the ability to build a
life that is best fit for you.

00:40:47.230 --> 00:40:48.321
So as an example,

00:40:48.321 --> 00:40:49.644
my one word is believe.

00:40:49.644 --> 00:40:51.477
We've talked about it.

00:40:52.806 --> 00:40:56.196
I want to bring believe in all situations.

00:40:56.196 --> 00:40:58.118
Believe gives me strength.

00:40:58.118 --> 00:41:00.629
Believe allows me to also find a path

00:41:00.629 --> 00:41:02.965
that may not be the same path

00:41:02.965 --> 00:41:04.988
that other people take
to solve that problem.

00:41:04.988 --> 00:41:06.669
So as an example,

00:41:06.669 --> 00:41:08.604
letting somebody go.

00:41:08.604 --> 00:41:10.952
It's important as an entrepreneur.

00:41:10.952 --> 00:41:11.785
- Hm.

00:41:11.785 --> 00:41:12.691
- It's great to think

00:41:12.691 --> 00:41:14.360
that everybody's just going
to work out, and hurray.

00:41:14.360 --> 00:41:16.675
But some people are going
to have to be let go,

00:41:16.675 --> 00:41:18.990
and sometimes that's your fault

00:41:18.990 --> 00:41:21.472
because your a bad manager
and you make the wrong hire,

00:41:21.472 --> 00:41:25.639
and sometimes it's their fault
for not doing their work.

00:41:26.637 --> 00:41:29.225
For someone who's positive and optimistic,

00:41:29.225 --> 00:41:30.743
how do you let somebody go?

00:41:30.743 --> 00:41:32.674
My first letting somebody go was,

00:41:32.674 --> 00:41:34.690
I was super scared about it.

00:41:34.690 --> 00:41:36.631
So I had to do it, 'cause
I'm scared about it,

00:41:36.631 --> 00:41:39.368
and it's the right thing to do.

00:41:39.368 --> 00:41:40.503
But then it challenged me.

00:41:40.503 --> 00:41:42.113
I think, okay, my one word is believe.

00:41:42.113 --> 00:41:45.509
How do I fire in a believe way?

00:41:45.509 --> 00:41:46.342
- Hm.

00:41:46.342 --> 00:41:48.081
- All the stuff that I saw about firing,

00:41:48.081 --> 00:41:49.366
I didn't like any of it.

00:41:49.366 --> 00:41:51.050
It was like, you need to fire on a Friday,

00:41:51.050 --> 00:41:54.017
so that they can't hack your computer

00:41:54.017 --> 00:41:55.524
or take stuff with them,

00:41:55.524 --> 00:41:57.146
or all of these reasons.

00:41:57.146 --> 00:41:59.070
Like you got to fire with somebody there,

00:41:59.070 --> 00:42:01.279
so there is a witness, and I don't know.

00:42:01.279 --> 00:42:02.112
Everything I read,

00:42:02.112 --> 00:42:03.469
and I read 'cause I was anxious.

00:42:03.469 --> 00:42:04.302
I was worried about it.

00:42:04.302 --> 00:42:06.239
I read all sorts of stuff on firing.

00:42:06.239 --> 00:42:09.335
And I didn't resonate with any of it.

00:42:09.335 --> 00:42:10.257
And so I knew I had to do it,

00:42:10.257 --> 00:42:11.983
but I didn't want to do
it in the way that I read.

00:42:11.983 --> 00:42:13.004
So I challenged myself to think

00:42:13.004 --> 00:42:14.496
okay, I'm all about believe,

00:42:14.496 --> 00:42:16.746
how do I fire with believe?

00:42:19.993 --> 00:42:22.200
And for me it was having
a conversation with them,

00:42:22.200 --> 00:42:25.533
and really just understanding
that this wasn't even

00:42:25.533 --> 00:42:28.420
the best fit for them, you know.

00:42:28.420 --> 00:42:29.939
- Hm, yep.

00:42:29.939 --> 00:42:31.700
- He wanted to be a baker.

00:42:31.700 --> 00:42:34.178
His heart of hearts, he
wanted to be a baker,

00:42:34.178 --> 00:42:38.043
and he's working for me
doing marketing stuff.

00:42:38.043 --> 00:42:41.819
And he loved me and the
environment and the positivity,

00:42:41.819 --> 00:42:44.620
but he wasn't doing the thing
that he was suppose to do.

00:42:44.620 --> 00:42:47.425
And he had nobody pushing
him to be a baker,

00:42:47.425 --> 00:42:50.776
and he was too afraid to take
that leap and go be a baker.

00:42:50.776 --> 00:42:53.026
And so, understanding that,

00:42:55.725 --> 00:42:57.685
it was about September when
we had this conversation.

00:42:57.685 --> 00:43:02.153
I said, listen, I'll work with
you 'til the end of the year,

00:43:02.153 --> 00:43:04.454
and then I want you to go and be a baker,

00:43:04.454 --> 00:43:06.111
'cause you want to be a baker.

00:43:06.111 --> 00:43:08.398
And however I can help you get there,

00:43:08.398 --> 00:43:09.325
if you need a reference.

00:43:09.325 --> 00:43:12.358
If you need me to come in
with you to the bake shop.

00:43:12.358 --> 00:43:14.871
Whatever it is, you're
suppose to go off and do that.

00:43:14.871 --> 00:43:17.269
You're holding yourself back doing here.

00:43:17.269 --> 00:43:20.087
And they can come across as self-serving

00:43:20.087 --> 00:43:22.422
and listening to it if you
just read it in the book,

00:43:22.422 --> 00:43:23.255
because sure, it helps me.

00:43:23.255 --> 00:43:24.395
The guy's not working for me anymore.

00:43:24.395 --> 00:43:25.228
It's great.

00:43:25.228 --> 00:43:26.587
I can get somebody else
who's meant for the position.

00:43:26.587 --> 00:43:29.670
But it comes from a genuine
love and care for him,

00:43:29.670 --> 00:43:31.919
because I believe in him and his potential

00:43:31.919 --> 00:43:33.842
to be something more
than what I can give him.

00:43:33.842 --> 00:43:36.483
I can't give him a baking
job working for me.

00:43:36.483 --> 00:43:38.533
It's just not going to happen.

00:43:38.533 --> 00:43:40.800
I'd love to stretch my
team and go into new areas,

00:43:40.800 --> 00:43:44.927
but the Evan Carmichael
bakery is not on the horizon

00:43:44.927 --> 00:43:46.760
for what I want to do.

00:43:48.019 --> 00:43:51.170
And so I discovered how
to fire in a believe way.

00:43:51.170 --> 00:43:52.291
- Hm.

00:43:52.291 --> 00:43:54.279
- And I think understanding
what your one word is,

00:43:54.279 --> 00:43:55.512
your core value,

00:43:55.512 --> 00:43:58.674
and then seeing everything that you do

00:43:58.674 --> 00:43:59.703
in a business context,

00:43:59.703 --> 00:44:01.395
as well as a personal context,

00:44:01.395 --> 00:44:03.134
through that lens helps...

00:44:03.134 --> 00:44:03.967
- Yeah.

00:44:03.967 --> 00:44:06.458
- Helps one, give you
the confidence to do it,

00:44:06.458 --> 00:44:07.688
and then helps you find your path

00:44:07.688 --> 00:44:10.781
that may be different than
everybody else's path.

00:44:10.781 --> 00:44:12.179
- Hm.

00:44:12.179 --> 00:44:15.063
- So that's a long answer,

00:44:15.063 --> 00:44:16.886
but those are my thoughts.

00:44:16.886 --> 00:44:18.815
I think that's super important.

00:44:18.815 --> 00:44:19.648
- I love it.

00:44:19.648 --> 00:44:22.481
That one word can become
like the barometer

00:44:22.481 --> 00:44:25.481
on what to measure or decide through

00:44:27.084 --> 00:44:29.167
all the different things.

00:44:33.544 --> 00:44:35.602
Tell me, you've clearly
found your passion.

00:44:35.602 --> 00:44:38.460
A lot of, it can be easy
when people really connect,

00:44:38.460 --> 00:44:40.937
like really align with that thing

00:44:40.937 --> 00:44:42.066
that they're really passionate about,

00:44:42.066 --> 00:44:45.540
then it is easier to then stay the course

00:44:45.540 --> 00:44:46.729
and to dig deep.

00:44:46.729 --> 00:44:48.055
If you're passionate,

00:44:48.055 --> 00:44:49.872
then there is really no other option.

00:44:49.872 --> 00:44:51.154
Even though you might
have the kind of wobbles

00:44:51.154 --> 00:44:52.444
in your head.

00:44:52.444 --> 00:44:53.552
How do people actually find

00:44:53.552 --> 00:44:54.933
what they're really passionate about?

00:44:54.933 --> 00:44:55.777
Because I think sometimes people

00:44:55.777 --> 00:44:57.844
can get a little bit overwhelmed

00:44:57.844 --> 00:45:01.729
by the conversation around
what am I here to do?

00:45:01.729 --> 00:45:03.456
And then it can get a
little bit over-thought.

00:45:03.456 --> 00:45:05.679
So how do people really connect into what

00:45:05.679 --> 00:45:06.823
they are passionate about?

00:45:06.823 --> 00:45:09.732
- Yeah, I think the two
most important exercises

00:45:09.732 --> 00:45:10.658
that people can go through

00:45:10.658 --> 00:45:13.922
is one, finding out what
they stand for, right?

00:45:13.922 --> 00:45:14.872
Finding their one word.

00:45:14.872 --> 00:45:17.153
Finding their believe system.

00:45:17.153 --> 00:45:20.236
And that is a lot of self-reflection,

00:45:22.070 --> 00:45:25.475
that is a lot of personal
time, a lot of thinking.

00:45:25.475 --> 00:45:27.032
- Hm.

00:45:27.032 --> 00:45:31.138
- You can't thing your way
to find your passion, though.

00:45:31.138 --> 00:45:33.076
You can take like different tests and say,

00:45:33.076 --> 00:45:35.576
well based on this, I'm
supposed to be a teacher,

00:45:35.576 --> 00:45:37.169
or whatever it is.

00:45:37.169 --> 00:45:39.620
And that might be start,

00:45:39.620 --> 00:45:44.390
but you can't think your
way into a relationship.

00:45:44.390 --> 00:45:45.688
You can't, you know, you're married,

00:45:45.688 --> 00:45:49.040
you can't just sit there
and put on a piece of paper

00:45:49.040 --> 00:45:51.436
here are the ten things
my husband has to have,

00:45:51.436 --> 00:45:53.367
and then poof, here he is,

00:45:53.367 --> 00:45:54.968
because when you get
into that relationship

00:45:54.968 --> 00:45:57.777
you're going to realize that two
of those things didn't matter,

00:45:57.777 --> 00:45:59.554
and you have to add four more to the list

00:45:59.554 --> 00:46:01.777
that now do matter to you

00:46:01.777 --> 00:46:03.732
that you didn't even realize before.

00:46:03.732 --> 00:46:08.176
And so, my advice in terms
of finding you passion,

00:46:08.176 --> 00:46:10.013
because it is one of the
most important things

00:46:10.013 --> 00:46:11.060
that you need to do.

00:46:11.060 --> 00:46:13.042
If you look at anybody that's
had success in their careers,

00:46:13.042 --> 00:46:15.541
they follow something
they're passionate about,

00:46:15.541 --> 00:46:17.707
is to try stuff.

00:46:17.707 --> 00:46:20.124
Is to explore, is to say yes.

00:46:22.284 --> 00:46:25.136
I own a salsa dance company.

00:46:25.136 --> 00:46:28.174
On paper it looks like a
really silly investment.

00:46:28.174 --> 00:46:31.759
When I first walked in
to a salsa dance class,

00:46:31.759 --> 00:46:33.860
it was just a fun thing to do,

00:46:33.860 --> 00:46:35.748
and on paper, I'm too tall.

00:46:35.748 --> 00:46:37.190
I don't speak spanish.

00:46:37.190 --> 00:46:38.766
I take big steps.

00:46:38.766 --> 00:46:40.287
I didn't hear the music growing up.

00:46:40.287 --> 00:46:43.287
Like all of these things against me,

00:46:44.673 --> 00:46:46.279
but I loved it.

00:46:46.279 --> 00:46:47.343
I had fun.

00:46:47.343 --> 00:46:49.285
I wanted to go back again.

00:46:49.285 --> 00:46:51.621
And so I wouldn't put so much pressure,

00:46:51.621 --> 00:46:52.454
If I put all that pressure to say

00:46:52.454 --> 00:46:54.014
I need to find my next business.

00:46:54.014 --> 00:46:54.847
This has to be it.

00:46:54.847 --> 00:46:56.865
I have to know on the
first try and that's it.

00:46:56.865 --> 00:46:59.515
Then I probably wouldn't even
have gone to that salsa class,

00:46:59.515 --> 00:47:01.272
and if I did, it would've
been so miserable

00:47:01.272 --> 00:47:05.439
because I'd be waiting for
it to be this amazing thing.

00:47:07.013 --> 00:47:08.171
And instead, I liked it

00:47:08.171 --> 00:47:10.248
and I wanted to come back and do it again.

00:47:10.248 --> 00:47:11.810
And I liked it and I wanted
to come back and do it again.

00:47:11.810 --> 00:47:14.119
And I continued to like,

00:47:14.119 --> 00:47:16.786
then I became a regular student.

00:47:17.777 --> 00:47:19.211
I became an investor,

00:47:19.211 --> 00:47:21.523
and then I bought the business
in January of this year,

00:47:21.523 --> 00:47:24.027
and it's the largest salsa
dancing school in North America.

00:47:24.027 --> 00:47:26.378
We'll teach five, six
thousand students a year

00:47:26.378 --> 00:47:27.961
how to dance salsa.

00:47:29.400 --> 00:47:33.498
And if I just didn't say
yes and take that one class,

00:47:33.498 --> 00:47:35.331
I wouldn't have known.

00:47:36.916 --> 00:47:37.977
- Hm.

00:47:37.977 --> 00:47:41.046
- And so, I think you
may have some inklings.

00:47:41.046 --> 00:47:43.135
It's like one, listen to that voice.

00:47:43.135 --> 00:47:45.074
Like, I want to say amen, say amen.

00:47:45.074 --> 00:47:46.741
You know, I want to,

00:47:48.315 --> 00:47:52.014
you pass by and you see
that sign for piano lessons.

00:47:52.014 --> 00:47:53.522
It's like, well that's interesting.

00:47:53.522 --> 00:47:54.789
Well take the thing and call the guy,

00:47:54.789 --> 00:47:57.078
and take a piano lesson and just see.

00:47:57.078 --> 00:47:58.022
You never know where it's going to lead.

00:47:58.022 --> 00:47:59.714
A lot times it doesn't make sense.

00:47:59.714 --> 00:48:01.633
It doesn't make sense looking forward.

00:48:01.633 --> 00:48:03.551
You can't figure out,

00:48:03.551 --> 00:48:07.343
like if you try to plan
your career with your head,

00:48:07.343 --> 00:48:09.055
you're never going to make it.

00:48:09.055 --> 00:48:10.450
'Cause your head only
understands the world

00:48:10.450 --> 00:48:11.464
that currently exists,

00:48:11.464 --> 00:48:14.236
where your heart can make
a new world for yourself.

00:48:14.236 --> 00:48:15.069
- Hm.

00:48:15.069 --> 00:48:17.147
- So you make the big decisions
in you life with your heart,

00:48:17.147 --> 00:48:18.017
and then you use your head

00:48:18.017 --> 00:48:19.901
to figure out how to make it happen.

00:48:19.901 --> 00:48:20.968
- Um-Hmm.

00:48:20.968 --> 00:48:24.616
- And so you can't think
your way to something

00:48:24.616 --> 00:48:27.606
that you need to make a
decision with the heart.

00:48:27.606 --> 00:48:29.969
And so it's saying yes to things.

00:48:29.969 --> 00:48:31.860
It's trying stuff out.

00:48:31.860 --> 00:48:33.740
Trying anything once.

00:48:33.740 --> 00:48:35.597
If you don't like it, that's okay.

00:48:35.597 --> 00:48:37.941
If you do it, then you keep doing it

00:48:37.941 --> 00:48:39.810
until you find the path that feel like,

00:48:39.810 --> 00:48:41.110
you know what, this is
the right thing to do.

00:48:41.110 --> 00:48:41.943
Now's the time.

00:48:41.943 --> 00:48:45.262
I want to devote more and
more and more time to this.

00:48:45.262 --> 00:48:46.500
I can't put this down.

00:48:46.500 --> 00:48:50.517
I love this so much I got to keep going.

00:48:50.517 --> 00:48:52.815
And then that's what you should be doing.

00:48:52.815 --> 00:48:55.821
And some people that journey
is longer than others,

00:48:55.821 --> 00:48:58.904
but the people who can't
find their passion,

00:48:58.904 --> 00:49:02.230
I would challenge them to
look at their activities,

00:49:02.230 --> 00:49:04.302
and I would say they're
not doing anything.

00:49:04.302 --> 00:49:07.407
They're going home and
watching T.V. shows,

00:49:07.407 --> 00:49:09.890
and have the same life.

00:49:09.890 --> 00:49:11.167
They're living the same boring life.

00:49:11.167 --> 00:49:12.511
They're not trying to do anything,

00:49:12.511 --> 00:49:14.428
and to try and think their way out of it

00:49:14.428 --> 00:49:17.428
they're trying to read an article on

00:49:18.651 --> 00:49:20.734
25 jobs to start in 2017,

00:49:21.666 --> 00:49:23.354
and that's what they're going to do.

00:49:23.354 --> 00:49:25.471
You got to try and feel it out.

00:49:25.471 --> 00:49:26.304
You got to do it.

00:49:26.304 --> 00:49:27.137
You got to say yes.

00:49:27.137 --> 00:49:27.970
You got to go to meet ups.

00:49:27.970 --> 00:49:32.137
You got to go to that event
that you've never been to,

00:49:34.349 --> 00:49:35.619
